# BOA Accreditation — read the docs before writing code

Read `docs/CURRENT-STATE.md` first, every session, before anything else. It is
the only document allowed to describe what is currently true. If this file and
that file disagree, that file wins.

`/docs` is canonical. These rules do not restate the architecture, because three
separate descriptions of one system is how the sibling project broke once
already.

| Need | File |
| --- | --- |
| What is done / not done / next | `docs/CURRENT-STATE.md` |
| What we are building and why | `docs/PRODUCT.md` |
| Structure, routing, layering | `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` |
| Schema, ledger, constraints | `docs/DATA-MODEL.md` |
| Tokens, grants, RLS, CSP | `docs/SECURITY.md` |
| The offline gate | `docs/OFFLINE-SYNC.md` |
| Any UI at all | `docs/DESIGN-SYSTEM.md` |
| Runbook, BMS loop, retention | `docs/OPERATIONS.md` |
| Why something is the way it is | `docs/DECISIONS.md` |
| Your task ID and its acceptance criteria | `docs/ROADMAP.md` |

## Commands

**`pnpm` is not on the PATH on this machine.** Every script runs as
`corepack pnpm <script>`. Bare `pnpm` fails with `command not found`. Never
hand the user a command starting with `pnpm`.

```
corepack pnpm typecheck && corepack pnpm lint && corepack pnpm test && corepack pnpm build
corepack pnpm test:visual   # design fidelity — required for ANY UI change
corepack pnpm test:db       # pgTAP, against a real PostgreSQL
corepack pnpm db:state      # ask the database, do not ask a document
```

